<section class="section" id="why">
  <div class="conteneur">
    <h2>Why move away from traditional AZERTY</h2>

    <div class="prose">
      <p>The AZERTY that Windows installs by default dates from a time when people wrote few
      accented capitals, no code, and rarely in another language. Three everyday uses are
      poorly served today.</p>
    </div>

    <ul class="pourquoi-liste">
      <li>
        <span class="pourquoi-liste__caracteres" aria-hidden="true">É Ç</span>
        <strong>Accented capitals take a detour.</strong>
        Typing “État”, “Ça” or “École” takes a detour through numeric codes or a character map.
        <a href="/e-aigu-majuscule" hreflang="fr">More about the É<span class="visuellement-cache"> (in French)</span></a>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="pourquoi-liste__caracteres" aria-hidden="true">«&nbsp;»</span>
        <strong>French typography is not on the French keyboard.</strong>
        French quotation marks, the em dash, nonbreaking spaces and the œ ligature are missing,
        though a thesis or a formal letter calls for them.
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="pourquoi-liste__caracteres" aria-hidden="true">{&nbsp;}</span>
        <strong>Programming symbols force you to stretch your hand.</strong>
        Braces, brackets, the vertical bar and the backslash are typed with <kbd>AltGr</kbd> on the
        number row.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <div class="prose pourquoi-afnor">
      <p>The AFNOR layout took a step in the right direction, but 31&nbsp;characters change
      key and its programming symbols remain hard to reach. AZERTY&nbsp;Global
      fixes the real problems while keeping everything that already works.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section" id="what-changes">
